hi there steve kaufman here um i want to  do a video here about this whole subject  of  people correcting others  when they speak or when they're learning  a foreign language uh i have had two  videos recently one in spanish one in  english which touch on  this theme  in both cases i responded to people who  wanted to correct or  criticize my ability in speaking one or  other foreign language  i don't really you know it doesn't  bother me that people correct me  or criticize me when i speak another  language it's just that i'm not  interested you know it doesn't destroy  my confidence i happen to be very  confident  in speaking uh foreign languages and  even if i speak very poorly  it doesn't bother me that i speak poorly  uh i normally tend not to speak when i'm  at the early stages of learning a  language but if i run into someone as i  do occasionally say  who's a korean speaker even though i  don't claim to speak korean and i can  only stumble around in korean i'll say  some things in korean just for fun  i know that when i speak russian i  struggle very pretty badly i struggle in  italian that i haven't spoken for a long  time i certainly struggle in portuguese  and i'll still speak it and if people  criticize me it doesn't really bother me  but i don't want it i'm not looking for  it i'm not asking for it i don't need it  it doesn't interest me because i know  that i will in time improve  and very often i'm quite aware of the  mistakes that i'm making  now the discussions that we had uh  are there some of the comments to my  videos  were along the lines of a number of  people said well why are you so  sensitive and what are you you know what  are you afraid of people just trying to  help you should accept  the corrections but i i find that  corrections don't help  uh  i cannot while i'm trying to speak and  there's a fair amount of pressure on you  when you're trying to speak in a foreign  language already you're forgetting  things that in fact you know because if  you spoke to yourself in a monologue you  would remember a number of the words  that you can't remember when you're now  all of a sudden in a situation where you  have to remember them so there's enough  pressure  and uh i can't  you know basically  process the corrections and i know this  very well myself i i was today i was  having a discussion in english with  someone and i corrected because they  asked for it i corrected one of their uh  structures in in english they said  something like uh  you know i can't remember what they were  trying to say the more you do this the  more you know the more you  study the better you get or something  and they just couldn't get it they had  some  form that came from their language and i  said no we say the more you do this the  easier it becomes well the very next  time they said it they got it wrong  again  i think it's much better that i put  those  corrections in a report that the person  then downloads that's how we do it at  link  and they then integrate it they import  it into their system save those phrases  and hopefully start to notice that  phrase so that slowly it becomes a part  of their  of the phrases that they can use  one person commented uh you know when i  said i don't  want to be corrected i don't think it's  useful to be corrected and they said  well you've said that  the process of learning a second  language is very similar to the process  whereby a child learns his or her first  language the parents are always  correcting the child therefore wouldn't  it make sense for the adult learner also  to be corrected  well i have a number of problems with  that position  first of all the adult in fact is not a  child the child is used to being  corrected  you know  for you know  whatever naughty things the child does  the child is knows if they are in a  position vis-a-vis the parent that the  parent is allowed and will in fact  constantly correct them and tell them  what to do that's fine different with  adults we're not somebody's child  and i think the idea that the  relationships in a classroom are one of  a mother  telling her children what to do is not  i don't think a very good one certainly  once we're adult learners even when  we're younger  but but the other thing is i don't think  that parents correcting their children  in learning their native language i  don't think that has much influence at  all on how a child speaks  i think that some parents will correct  their children others do not  and all children end up speaking more or  less the same  so that i don't think it has any  any influence at all on on the child's  uh language or ability to speak the  native language correctly i think what  matters much much more is the experience  of the child in speaking with other  children  and possibly eventually at school  when they get into  you know higher levels if you want of  the language i think the child will  absorb a lot of words  from parents  and if the parents speak with the child  a lot and speak intelligently and treat  the child  as  you know a member of their conversation  not necessarily by correcting them at  every step but by including them in  their conversation that this will  encourage the child's language skills  and introduce the child to lots of words  and enrich their vocabulary so i think  that's a good thing to do but i think  correcting the child every time they  make a mistake in the language is  essentially useless because  the child  starts to pick up on certain patterns in  the language and starts to make some of  its own rules  so  you know if the past of walk is walked  and past of talk is talked  then the past of go is goad  so it's perfectly normal for the child  to say gold  and if you don't correct the child the  child will eventually figure out that is  went  and the correction by the parent is  essentially a waste of time  proof of that is that we have immigrant  children who arrive let's say in canada  as was the case with me i arrived at the  age of five  and i didn't learn english from my  parents  very often they were not in a position  to correct me although their english  wasn't bad  and i suspect there are lots of  immigrant children who arrive at the age  of three four five six seven whose  parents don't speak english at all  and yet if those kids go to school with  english-speaking kids they'll speak  english quite correctly very very  quickly  uh without the need for any correction  so the idea that the parents correct  them uh i think it's just simply  not true or at least it may be true for  some parents but it's quite irrelevant  the  most important thing in language  learning is the motivation and the  attitude of the learner  that the learner have positive feelings  towards the language and for the  experience of learning the language  there's increasing evidence and i don't  have it handy but i'm reading a book in  portuguese which means i don't  understand it a hundred percent but it  talks about our emotions and feelings  and so forth  damascus i think is i can't remember  but um  the whole process of learning involves  our emotions it's not something that's  done in isolation by some part of our  brain it's very much  you know  a part of how we respond to  to stimulus  and  and of course if we have good feelings  about something  uh positive feelings we're gonna do  better we're gonna feel better we're  gonna you know all kinds of positive  whatever chemicals charge around in our  brain so the big issue is to me  the correction does it make the learner  feel better that's the biggest issue of  all does it encourage the learner if a  learner is encouraged by a correction  then correct the learner and realize  though that it's not the substance of  your correction that's going to help the  learner but rather that that learner for  whatever reason is encouraged by  corrections it makes him happy or her  turns them on makes them feel positive  about the language but i suspect those  people are the minority i think a  majority of people  if they're trying to communicate with  someone and if they haven't asked to be  corrected  then for you to correct them is actually  disturbing because it tells them that  you are not interested in what they have  to say you are more interested in  correcting how they say it  whereas the person who's speaking in a  foreign language actually is more  interested in or should be in what they  have to communicate  so  i say again on the subject of  corrections unless you have the specific  a specific request from someone to  correct them  the permission  of the learner or non-native speaker you  should not correct them regardless of  the number of mistakes they make and  even when they ask you to correct them i  would be  i would go very carefully in terms of  correcting occasionally and far better  in writing than interrupting them when  they're speaking and that's why we send  reports after discussion that link and  we try not to too much correct people  while they're speaking so there you have  it that's my take on the subject of  correcting look forward to hearing from  you
 

